And when you have it ready, send it out ASAP to our sub-branch.

282
00:10:50,110 --> 00:10:50,550
Sure.

283
00:11:03,380 --> 00:11:05,160
I think I broke this thing.

284
00:11:05,620 --> 00:11:06,880
Maxine, can you help me out here?

285
00:11:07,180 --> 00:11:08,340
I'm not really a tech guy.

286
00:11:09,540 --> 00:11:10,260
Yeah, sure.

287
00:11:10,580 --> 00:11:12,020
I think it's just out of toner.

288
00:11:12,420 --> 00:11:14,220
You can go use the other one upstairs.

289
00:11:14,780 --> 00:11:18,440
On your way up, can you fax this while I try and fix this thing?

290
00:11:19,780 --> 00:11:20,140
Sure.

291
00:11:26,050 --> 00:11:26,630
Damn it!

292
00:11:27,210 --> 00:11:28,970
Everything in this office seems to be breaking down.

293
00:11:29,900 --> 00:11:31,460
I'll send the stupid fax later.

294
00:11:37,400 --> 00:11:37,900
Oh great!

295
00:11:38,320 --> 00:11:39,980
Is someone playing a practical joke on me?

296
00:11:40,080 --> 00:11:40,520
This is ridiculous!

297
00:11:42,140 --> 00:11:43,620
The elevator has some sort of malfunction.

298
00:11:44,340 --> 00:11:45,200
Just take the stairs dude.

299
00:11:45,660 --> 00:11:46,620
What floor are you going to?

300
00:11:47,430 --> 00:11:49,110
I have to go up 15 floors!

301
00:11:49,970 --> 00:11:50,590
Never mind.

302
00:12:00,760 --> 00:12:01,180
Made it.

303
00:12:02,100 --> 00:12:09,100
There's the copier.

304
00:12:09,100 --> 00:12:16,100
No!

305
00:12:16,100 --> 00:12:26,980
Alright, so Erica, everything that you touch that's electronic seems to malfunction, right?

306
00:12:26,980 --> 00:12:28,100
This is absolutely true.

307
00:12:29,100 --> 00:12:35,340
I don't know what it is, but you know what, if I turn on a computer it will suddenly get a virus.

308
00:12:36,660 --> 00:12:38,760
And even if I'm very careful it still breaks.

309
00:12:39,180 --> 00:12:39,320
Right.

310
00:12:39,840 --> 00:12:40,720
Yeah, it happens sometimes.

311
00:12:40,920 --> 00:12:41,780
I can relate to that.

312
00:12:42,660 --> 00:12:47,060
But you are much better at computers and technology than I am.

313
00:12:47,660 --> 00:12:49,360
Well, I really, really like them.

314
00:12:49,780 --> 00:12:56,660
And well, I guess even I know a little bit about copy machines or copiers because my uncles are like copy technicians.

315
00:12:57,100 --> 00:12:58,100
Really?

316
00:12:58,100 --> 00:13:07,960
Yeah, so they fix copiers that broke down or they replace toner and they do all that stuff so that's why I know a little bit about it.

317
00:13:08,460 --> 00:13:10,540
But actually the original term is a Xerox machine.

318
00:13:10,940 --> 00:13:11,780
Yes, why is that?

319
00:13:11,980 --> 00:13:12,660
From the brand, right?

320
00:13:12,940 --> 00:13:13,440
Ah, right.

321
00:13:13,800 --> 00:13:20,200
There's a brand called Xerox and Xerox invented and patented this technology of making copies.

322
00:13:20,820 --> 00:13:25,260
Alright well the next time my photocopier explodes I'll call you.

323
00:13:25,260 --> 00:13:27,380
Call my uncles, right?

324
00:13:27,380 --> 00:13:36,060
Alright and well let us know how things in your office break down or if you have any stories of like Erica that breaks everything she touches.

325
00:13:36,060 --> 00:13:36,240
It's true.

326
00:13:37,060 --> 00:13:44,540
Alright, so come to our website, come to our comment section and leave your opinions and we'll be there to also answer any doubts or questions you might have.

327
00:13:44,900 --> 00:13:45,020
That's right.

328
00:13:45,900 --> 00:13:50,120
You can find us at EnglishPod.com and Marco and I are always around.

329
00:13:50,660 --> 00:13:51,860
Alright so we'll see you guys there.

330
00:13:52,140 --> 00:13:54,020
Yep, we will.

331
00:13:54,020 --> 00:13:56,420
Okay guys, thanks for listening and until next time.

332
00:13:57,000 --> 00:13:57,140
Bye.

333
